   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Portfolio Input Section\n",
    "\n",
    "Edit the tickers, investment amount, and date range here.\n",
    "\n",
    "Default portfolio:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Apple: AAPL\n",
    "- Microsoft: MSFT\n",
    "- Nvidia: NVDA\n",
    "- JPMorgan Chase: JPM\n",
    "- Exxon Mobil: XOM"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Portfolio Inputs\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "# Example stock tickers\n",
    "portfolio_tickers = ['AAPL', 'MSFT', 'NVDA', 'JPM', 'XOM']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Starting investment amount\n",
    "starting_investment = 10000\n",
    "\n",
    "# Date range for historical analysis\n",
    "start_date = '2020-01-01'\n",
    "end_date = None  # None means latest available data\n",
    "\n",
    "# Equal-weight portfolio allocation\n",
    "# Each stock receives the same percentage of the portfolio\n",
    "weights = np.array([1 / len(portfolio_tickers)] * len(portfolio_tickers))\n",
    "\n",
    "portfolio_settings = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Ticker': portfolio_tickers,\n",
    "    'Weight': weights,\n",
    "    'Investment Amount': weights * starting_investment\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "portfolio_settings"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "An equal-weight portfolio means the money is split evenly across all selected stocks. With five stocks and $10,000, each stock receives $2,000."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Stock Data Collection Using yfinance\n",
    "\n",
    "This section downloads historical stock prices from Yahoo Finance using the `yfinance` Python library."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Download Stock Price Data\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "# Download adjusted close prices\n",
    "raw_data = yf.download(\n",
    "    portfolio_tickers,\n",
    "    start=start_date,\n",
    "    end=end_date,\n",
    "    auto_adjust=True,\n",
    "    progress=False\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Select closing prices\n",
    "if isinstance(raw_data.columns, pd.MultiIndex):\n",
    "    price_data = raw_data['Close']\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    price_data = raw_data[['Close']]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Drop rows with missing values\n",
    "price_data = price_data.dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show the first few rows\n",
    "price_data.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "Stock prices change every trading day. Historical price data lets us measure past performance, risk, and portfolio behavior."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. Historical Returns Calculation\n",
    "\n",
    "Returns measure how much each stock gained or lost over time."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Calculate Daily Returns\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "daily_returns = price_data.pct_change().dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Annualized average return assumes about 252 trading days per year\n",
    "annual_returns = daily_returns.mean() * 252\n",
    "\n",
    "returns_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Annualized Return': annual_returns,\n",
    "    'Annualized Return (%)': annual_returns * 100\n",
    "}).sort_values(by='Annualized Return', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "returns_summary"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "Daily return shows the percentage gain or loss from one trading day to the next. Annualized return converts average daily performance into an estimated yearly return."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 8. Risk and Volatility Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Volatility measures how much a stock moves up and down. Higher volatility usually means higher risk."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Calculate Volatility\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "annual_volatility = daily_returns.std() * np.sqrt(252)\n",
    "\n",
    "risk_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Annualized Volatility': annual_volatility,\n",
    "    'Annualized Volatility (%)': annual_volatility * 100\n",
    "}).sort_values(by='Annualized Volatility', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "risk_summary"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Volatility Bar Chart\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = px.bar(\n",
    "    risk_summary.reset_index(),\n",
    "    x='Ticker' if 'Ticker' in risk_summary.reset_index().columns else risk_summary.reset_index().columns[0],\n",
    "    y='Annualized Volatility (%)',\n",
    "    title='Annualized Volatility by Stock',\n",
    "    labels={'Annualized Volatility (%)': 'Volatility (%)'}\n",
    ")\n",
    "fig.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "A stock with high volatility can rise fast, but it can also fall fast. Risk is not automatically bad, but investors should know what they are taking on."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 9. Portfolio Allocation Breakdown\n",
    "\n",
    "This section shows how the $10,000 investment is split across the selected stocks."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Portfolio Allocation Table\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "allocation_df = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Ticker': portfolio_tickers,\n",
    "    'Weight': weights,\n",
    "    'Weight (%)': weights * 100,\n",
    "    'Dollar Allocation': weights * starting_investment\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "allocation_df"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Portfolio Allocation Pie Chart\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = px.pie(\n",
    "    allocation_df,\n",
    "    names='Ticker',\n",
    "    values='Dollar Allocation',\n",
    "    title='Portfolio Allocation Breakdown'\n",
    ")\n",
    "fig.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "Allocation shows where the money is placed. A portfolio concentrated in only one or two stocks may carry more company-specific risk."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 10. Correlation Heatmap\n",
    "\n",
    "Correlation shows how closely stocks move together.\n",
    "\n",
    "- A correlation near 1 means stocks move very similarly.\n",
    "- A correlation near 0 means stocks do not move closely together.\n",
    "- A negative correlation means they often move in opposite directions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Correlation Matrix\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation_matrix = daily_returns.corr()\n",
    "correlation_matrix"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Correlation Heatmap\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(correlation_matrix, annot=True, cmap='coolwarm', linewidths=0.5)\n",
    "plt.title('Stock Return Correlation Heatmap')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "Diversification works better when stocks do not all move the same way. If every stock is highly correlated, the portfolio may drop together during market stress."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 11. Cumulative Return Chart\n",
    "\n",
    "Cumulative return shows how much an investment would have grown over time."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Cumulative Returns\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "cumulative_returns = (1 + daily_returns).cumprod()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Portfolio cumulative return using weights\n",
    "portfolio_daily_returns = daily_returns.dot(weights)\n",
    "portfolio_cumulative_returns = (1 + portfolio_daily_returns).cumprod()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert to dollar value\n",
    "portfolio_value = starting_investment * portfolio_cumulative_returns\n",
    "\n",
    "cumulative_returns.tail()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Cumulative Return Chart\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = go.Figure()\n",
    "\n",
    "for ticker in portfolio_tickers:\n",
    "    fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
    "        x=cumulative_returns.index,\n",
    "        y=cumulative_returns[ticker],\n",
    "        mode='lines',\n",
    "        name=ticker\n",
    "    ))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(\n",
    "    x=portfolio_cumulative_returns.index,\n",
    "    y=portfolio_cumulative_returns,\n",
    "    mode='lines',\n",
    "    name='Portfolio',\n",
    "    line=dict(width=4)\n",
    "))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.update_layout(\n",
    "    title='Cumulative Returns: Stocks vs Portfolio',\n",
    "    xaxis_title='Date',\n",
    "    yaxis_title='Growth of $1 Invested',\n",
    "    hovermode='x unified'\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Portfolio Value Chart\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = px.line(\n",
    "    x=portfolio_value.index,\n",
    "    y=portfolio_value.values,\n",
    "    title=f'Portfolio Value Over Time: ${starting_investment:,.0f} Initial Investment',\n",
    "    labels={'x': 'Date', 'y': 'Portfolio Value ($)'}\n",
    ")\n",
    "fig.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "Cumulative return helps investors see the big picture. Instead of focusing on one day, it shows how wealth changed over the full time period."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 12. Sharpe Ratio Calculation\n",
    "\n",
    "The Sharpe ratio measures return compared to risk.\n",
    "\n",
    "A higher Sharpe ratio generally means better risk-adjusted performance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Sharpe Ratio Calculation\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "# Assumed annual risk-free rate\n",
    "risk_free_rate = 0.04\n",
    "\n",
    "portfolio_annual_return = portfolio_daily_returns.mean() * 252\n",
    "portfolio_annual_volatility = portfolio_daily_returns.std() * np.sqrt(252)\n",
    "\n",
    "portfolio_sharpe_ratio = (portfolio_annual_return - risk_free_rate) / portfolio_annual_volatility\n",
    "\n",
    "sharpe_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Metric': ['Portfolio Annual Return', 'Portfolio Annual Volatility', 'Risk-Free Rate', 'Sharpe Ratio'],\n",
    "    'Value': [portfolio_annual_return, portfolio_annual_volatility, risk_free_rate, portfolio_sharpe_ratio]\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "sharpe_summary"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "The Sharpe ratio asks: \u201cWas the return worth the risk?\u201d A portfolio can have high returns, but if risk is extremely high, the quality of those returns may be weaker."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 13. Best and Worst Performing Stocks\n",
    "\n",
    "This section ranks the stocks by total return over the selected time period."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Best and Worst Performing Stocks\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "total_returns = (price_data.iloc[-1] / price_data.iloc[0]) - 1\n",
    "\n",
    "performance_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    'Total Return': total_returns,\n",
    "    'Total Return (%)': total_returns * 100,\n",
    "    'Starting Price': price_data.iloc[0],\n",
    "    'Ending Price': price_data.iloc[-1]\n",
    "}).sort_values(by='Total Return', ascending=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "best_stock = performance_summary.index[0]\n",
    "worst_stock = performance_summary.index[-1]\n",
    "\n",
    "performance_summary"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Performance Bar Chart\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "chart_df = performance_summary.reset_index()\n",
    "first_col = chart_df.columns[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = px.bar(\n",
    "    chart_df,\n",
    "    x=first_col,\n",
    "    y='Total Return (%)',\n",
    "    title='Total Return by Stock',\n",
    "    labels={first_col: 'Ticker', 'Total Return (%)': 'Total Return (%)'}\n",
    ")\n",
    "fig.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f'Best performing stock: {best_stock}')\n",
    "print(f'Worst performing stock: {worst_stock}')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "The best-performing stock created the most growth over the period. The worst-performing stock may still be useful if it lowers risk or improves diversification."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 14. Portfolio Insights\n",
    "\n",
    "This section turns the portfolio results into a short written summary. The goal is to explain the numbers clearly instead of only showing tables and charts.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "# Portfolio Insights\n",
    "# -----------------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "highest_vol_stock = risk_summary.index[0]\n",
    "lowest_vol_stock = risk_summary.index[-1]\n",
    "final_portfolio_value = portfolio_value.iloc[-1]\n",
    "portfolio_total_return = (final_portfolio_value / starting_investment) - 1\n",
    "\n",
    "insights = f'''\n",
    "Portfolio Insights\n",
    "------------------\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Portfolio Performance:\n",
    "The portfolio grew from ${starting_investment:,.2f} to ${final_portfolio_value:,.2f}, producing a total return of {portfolio_total_return:.2%} over the selected period.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Best Performer:\n",
    "{best_stock} was the strongest performer in the portfolio. This stock contributed positively to long-term growth and helped increase the overall portfolio value.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Weakest Performer:\n",
    "{worst_stock} had the lowest total return during the selected period. This does not automatically mean it is a bad investment, but it should be reviewed against the investor's goals.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Risk Profile:\n",
    "{highest_vol_stock} had the highest volatility, meaning it experienced the largest price swings. {lowest_vol_stock} had the lowest volatility, making it relatively more stable compared with the other holdings.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. Diversification:\n",
    "The portfolio includes technology, financial services, and energy exposure. This is stronger than holding only one sector, but the portfolio may still be heavily influenced by large-cap U.S. stocks.\n",
    "\n",
    "6. Sharpe Ratio:\n",
    "The portfolio Sharpe ratio is {portfolio_sharpe_ratio:.2f}. This measures how much return the portfolio earned for each unit of risk. A higher number is generally better.\n",
    "\n",
    "7. Business Summary:\n",
    "Overall, this portfolio is growth-oriented because it includes major technology companies such as Apple, Microsoft, and Nvidia, while JPMorgan and Exxon Mobil add financial and energy sector exposure.\n",
    "'''\n",
    "\n",
    "print(insights)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Finance meaning\n",
    "Written insights help turn raw calculations into decisions. In finance, the numbers matter, but the explanation is what makes the analysis useful.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 15. Final Dashboard-Style Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "This table summarizes the most important portfolio metrics in one place."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
